.\" Manual page for hashsync, in mdoc(7) format. docs/DESIGN.md specifies the
.\" behavior described here, so update both when a command, an option, or the
.\" configuration file changes. Tests in src/main.rs and src/config.rs check
.\" that this page documents every command, option, and configuration key,
.\" and no others, and the Nix build lints it with mandoc -T lint.
.Dd October 26, 2026
.Dt HASHSYNC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hashsync
.Nd run a command over SSH, deduplicating its output or input against indexed files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -profile Ar name | Fl -config Ar path
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl -no-human-readable
.Ar subcommand
.Op Ar argument ...
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm update
.Op Fl -compact
.Op Fl -rebuild
.Op Fl -scan-immutable
.Op Fl j Ar threads
.Nm
.Cm stats
.Nm
.Cm prune
.Op Fl -scan-immutable
.Nm
.Cm compact
.Nm
.Cm ssh
.Op Ar options
.Op Fl l Ar user
.Oo Ar user Ns @ Oc Ns Ar host
.Op Fl -
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Cm bench
.Op Fl -block-size Ar size
.Op Fl -probe-threads Ar threads
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm Cm ssh
runs a command on a remote host, much like
.Xr ssh 1 ,
and deduplicates its standard output, its standard input, or both: data that
the receiving host already has in its files is sent as a reference to a block
of one of those files.
By default, the command's standard output is deduplicated against files on the
local host
.Pq a Em pull ;
with
.Fl -dedup-stdin ,
standard input is deduplicated against files on the remote host instead
.Pq a Em push .
Blocks are deduplicated at any offset, so this saves bandwidth whenever a stream
repeats data that the receiving host already has, as with
.Xr tar 1
archives,
.Xr rsync 1
transfers, or package trees.
.Pp
The local host is the
.Em client
and the remote host the
.Em server .
The host that receives a deduplicated stream is its
.Em receiver ,
and the other host its
.Em sender .
The receiver keeps an
.Em index
of the blocks in the regular files under the
.Em roots
listed in its configuration file
.Pq see Sx CONFIGURATION .
.Nm Cm update
creates the index or brings it up to date, and so does
.Nm Cm ssh
when it starts, unless
.Fl -no-update
is given.
The receiver sends the sender its
.Em hash list ,
short hashes of all its blocks, which the sender looks for at every offset of
the stream.
Every match is verified against the receiver's files, so a file that changed
since the last update only reduces deduplication; the output always matches the
original stream exactly.
Both hosts cache hash lists, so later transfers only send the changes.
.Pp
The client starts
.Nm
on the server over two SSH sessions, which run the internal commands
.Nm Cm serve
and
.Nm Cm attach .
The server's shell looks
.Nm
up in its
.Ev PATH ,
unless
.Fl -hashsync-path
gives its location.
.Pp
The following options apply to all commands, and can be given before or after
the command name:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -config Ar path
Use the configuration file at
.Ar path
instead of a profile.
Unless the file sets
.Cm index_path ,
the index is named after the file, as for a profile, so files with the same
name share an index.
.It Fl -no-human-readable
Print byte counts in bytes instead of with units such as KiB, and the hash list
sizes of
.Cm bench
as plain numbers.
.It Fl -profile Ar name
Use the profile
.Ar name :
the configuration file
.Pa ~/.config/hashsync/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .toml ,
with the index
.Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .db
unless the file sets
.Cm index_path .
The name must not be empty or contain a slash.
Without
.Fl -profile
or
.Fl -config ,
the profile
.Ql default
is used.
The two options cannot be combined, and
.Cm bench
accepts neither.
.It Fl q , -quiet
Print only warnings and errors.
.El
.Pp
.Fl h
or
.Fl -help
prints a short help text, for a specific command if given after its name, and
.Fl V
or
.Fl -version
prints the version.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Cm update
.Op Fl -compact
.Op Fl -rebuild
.Op Fl -scan-immutable
.Op Fl j Ar threads
.Xc
Create the index, or bring it up to date: hash new and changed files, and
remove from the index the files and roots that no longer exist or cannot be
reached, or that the configuration no longer includes.
Files whose identity, size and change time are unchanged are not read again,
and fully indexed immutable roots are not scanned at all.
If
.Cm block_size
or
.Cm strong_hash
changed, the index is rebuilt.
Files and directories that cannot be read are skipped with a warning.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -compact
Compact the index at the end, like
.Cm compact .
.It Fl -rebuild
Rebuild the index from scratch.
.It Fl -scan-immutable
Also rescan fully indexed immutable roots, for example after their contents,
.Cm exclude
patterns or
.Cm one_filesystem
setting changed.
.It Fl j Ar threads
Number of threads that scan and hash, overriding the
.Cm threads
setting; 0 selects the default.
.El
.It Cm stats
Print statistics about the index, and warn if its settings differ from the
configuration.
The index must already exist.
.It Xo
.Cm prune
.Op Fl -scan-immutable
.Xc
Remove from the index the files and roots that no longer exist or cannot be
reached, or that the configuration no longer includes, without hashing
anything, then compact the index.
An immutable root stays as long as any of its copies exists
.Pq see Cm identifier .
The index must already exist and match the configuration.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -scan-immutable
Also check the individual paths of immutable roots.
.El
.It Cm compact
Compact the index by rewriting its database files without the data that
updates and prunes have removed.
The index must already exist and match the configuration.
.It Xo
.Cm ssh
.Op Ar options
.Op Fl l Ar user
.Oo Ar user Ns @ Oc Ns Ar host
.Op Fl -
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Xc
Run
.Ar command
on
.Ar host
over SSH, deduplicating its standard output, its standard input, or both.
.Nm Cm ssh
passes its standard input to the command, copies the command's standard output
and standard error back, and exits with the command's exit status.
It accepts the arguments that
.Xr rsync 1
passes to its remote shell, so
.Ql rsync -e 'hashsync ssh'
pulls with deduplication, and
.Ql rsync -e 'hashsync ssh --dedup-stdin'
pushes with it.
As with
.Xr ssh 1 ,
options may also follow
.Ar host ,
and the command and its arguments are joined into a single line, which the
server runs with the user's shell
.Po Ev SHELL ,
or
.Pa /bin/sh
if it is unset
.Pc .
A command that starts with
.Ql -
must follow
.Fl - .
.Pp
Both streams start immediately, while each receiver updates its index and sends
its hash list, so deduplication improves as the list arrives.
Each stream is buffered on both hosts in temporary files, so a slow reader does
not hold up the command or the transfer until the buffers are full.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -batch-stdin
Send standard input in full buffers rather than as soon as data is available.
This compresses better and deduplicates more blocks, but a program that waits
for replies, such as
.Xr rsync 1 ,
would wait forever.
.It Fl -batch-stdout
Send the command's standard output in full buffers, like
.Fl -batch-stdin .
.It Fl -compact
Compact each index after updating it, like
.Nm Cm update Fl -compact .
.It Fl -compression-level Ar level
The zstd compression level, from 1 to 19, or 0 to disable compression.
The default is 9.
.It Fl -compression-threads Ar threads
Number of zstd worker threads for each stream.
The default, 0, uses half the cores of the compressing host.
.It Fl -dedup-stdin
Deduplicate standard input against the server's index.
Without
.Fl -dedup-stdout ,
the command's standard output is only compressed.
.It Fl -dedup-stdout
Deduplicate the command's standard output against the client's index.
This is the default without
.Fl -dedup-stdin .
.It Fl -hashsync-path Ar path
The
.Nm
executable on the server, such as a statically linked binary on a host where
.Nm
is not installed: a name to look up in
.Ev PATH ,
or a path, absolute or relative to the remote home directory.
It is a single word, not a shell command; use a wrapper script to set its
environment.
The default is
.Ql hashsync .
.It Fl l Ar user
Log in as
.Ar user
on the server.
.It Fl -lookahead Ar size
How far ahead of the data already sent the sender looks for the receiver's
blocks.
It must be at least the receiver's block size, and the sender uses about twice
this much memory.
The default is 256\~MiB or three blocks, whichever is larger.
.It Fl -negative-cache Ar size
Size of the sender's cache of hashes that the receiver did not have, which
saves the sender from asking again about repeated data.
0 disables the cache.
The default is 128\~MiB.
.It Fl -no-compression-long
Disable zstd's long-distance matching, which finds repeated data up to 128\~MiB
apart.
.It Fl -no-sender-cache
Do not cache hash lists on the sender, and remove those cached for this
receiver and index, so that the complete list is sent every time.
.It Fl s , -no-shell
Run the command and its arguments as separate words, without a shell.
With
.Xr rsync 1 ,
use this only together with rsync's own
.Fl s
option; otherwise the server would take rsync's shell escapes literally.
.It Fl n , -no-stdin
Give the command an empty standard input, like
.Ql ssh -n ,
and do not read standard input.
This is needed in the background of an interactive shell, or under
.Xr nohup 1 .
Cannot be combined with
.Fl -dedup-stdin
or
.Fl -batch-stdin .
.It Fl -no-update
Do not update the indexes, and open them read-only, so that other readers can
use them at the same time.
Each index must exist and match its configuration.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl -compact
or
.Fl -scan-immutable .
.It Fl -probe-threads Ar threads
Number of threads with which the sender looks for the receiver's blocks in the
stream.
The default, 0, uses half the cores of the sender.
.It Fl -scan-immutable
Also rescan fully indexed immutable roots when updating each index, like
.Nm Cm update Fl -scan-immutable .
.It Fl -server-config Ar path
Use the configuration file at
.Ar path
on the server, absolute or relative to the remote home directory, instead of
its default profile.
.It Fl -server-profile Ar name
Use the profile
.Ar name
on the server instead of its default profile.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl -server-config .
.It Fl -spool-limit Ar size | Cm unlimited
Maximum amount of data buffered for each stream on each host.
The default is 2\~GiB.
.It Fl -ssh Ar ssh-command
The SSH command, split into words like a shell would, but without expansion.
The default is
.Ql ssh .
.Nm
adds options of its own, such as
.Ql -T
and
.Ql -o Compression=no ;
per-host settings belong in
.Xr ssh_config 5 .
.It Fl -wait
Make each deduplicated stream wait for the complete hash list, so that it sends
as few bytes as possible, however long the receiver's update takes.
.El
.Pp
Unless it fails itself,
.Nm Cm ssh
prints a summary to standard error at the end: for each stream, the bytes read
and sent and how they were deduplicated, including the matches that the
receiver rejected, either as hash collisions
.Pq Em false positives
or because the block failed verification, for example because its file changed
.Pq Em not found ;
then the total traffic and time.
.It Xo
.Cm bench
.Op Fl -block-size Ar size
.Op Fl -probe-threads Ar threads
.Xc
Measure how fast this host computes the hashes used for deduplication and
looks for indexed blocks in a stream, on one thread and on several, to help
choose
.Cm strong_hash ,
.Cm block_size
and
.Fl -probe-threads .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -block-size Ar size
Measure only blocks of
.Ar size ,
instead of a few common sizes, and look for blocks of that size instead of the
default
.Cm block_size .
.It Fl -probe-threads Ar threads
Number of threads that look for blocks, as for
.Nm Cm ssh .
.El
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
All commands except
.Cm bench
read a configuration file in TOML format, by default
.Pa ~/.config/hashsync/default.toml ,
the file of the profile
.Ql default .
.Fl -profile
and
.Fl -config
select another file; for
.Nm Cm ssh ,
they select the client's file, and
.Fl -server-profile
and
.Fl -server-config
select the server's.
A configuration file only describes its own host.
.Nm Cm ssh
needs one only on a host that receives a deduplicated stream, but a file named
by an option must exist.
Unknown keys are errors.
.Pp
Each profile has its own index, and
.Nm Cm ssh
deduplicates each stream against a single index.
Data sets that share no content and are transferred separately, such as a
Nix store and a photo collection, are best indexed in separate profiles.
Updates are then faster, hash lists are smaller, and instances of
.Nm Cm ssh
that deduplicate against different profiles do not wait for each other
.Pq see Sx CAVEATS .
.Pp
The following keys are recognized, all optional:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm block_size
Size of the indexed blocks, at least 512\~bytes; at least 4\~KiB is recommended.
Larger blocks make the index and the hash list smaller, but deduplicate less
data.
Changing it rebuilds the index.
The default is
.Ql 16KiB .
.It Cm candidate_attempts
How many indexed blocks with a matching hash the receiver tries before
rejecting a match, at least 1.
All copies of an immutable root count as one.
The default is 128.
.It Cm hashlist_max_age
How long this host keeps unused cached hash lists, and the temporary files
that
.Nm
leaves when it fails: a number of seconds, or a number followed by
.Ql s ,
.Ql m ,
.Ql h
or
.Ql d ,
at least 1 second.
A value below an hour gets a warning, since expiry could then remove the
temporary files of a
.Nm Cm ssh
that is still running.
The default is
.Ql 30d .
.It Cm index_path
Directory of the index, a RocksDB database.
The default is
.Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .db
for the file
.Ar name Ns Pa .toml .
.It Cm roots
The roots to index, as an array of tables written
.Ql [[roots]] ,
with the keys described below.
.It Cm strong_hash
Hash used to verify blocks:
.Ql blake3 ,
the default, or
.Ql sha256 .
Changing it rebuilds the index.
.It Cm threads
Number of threads that scan and hash files in an update, or look up paths in a
prune.
The default, 0, uses four per core, but at least 16 and at most 64.
.It Cm tmp_data_dir
Directory for the temporary files of
.Nm Cm ssh ,
which disappear when it exits.
Put it on a local disk; on tmpfs, the data would take up memory.
The default is
.Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/tmp-data .
.El
.Pp
Each root accepts the following keys:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm path
An absolute path, or a glob pattern of absolute paths; each match, a directory
or a regular file, is a root.
.Ql * ,
.Ql \&?
and bracket expressions such as
.Ql [a-z]
match within a path component, and
.Ql **
across components.
Matching compares bytes, is case-sensitive, and includes names that start with
a dot.
Symbolic links are not followed, and files and directories that cannot be
reached do not match; they get a warning, like patterns without matches.
Two roots with the same path, or one inside the other, are an error.
An update also fails if a mount makes it reach the same directory twice, as
with a bind mount of a directory that a root contains, or of a parent
directory; exclude the mount point, or set
.Cm one_filesystem
for a mount below a root.
This key is required.
.It Cm exclude
Patterns of files and directories to leave out, together with everything below
them.
A pattern that starts with
.Ql /
matches absolute paths; any other pattern matches names, and cannot contain a
slash.
.It Cm identifier
How an immutable root names its
.Em group :
matches with the same identifier are
.Em copies
of the same content, which the index stores once.
.Ql \(dqpath\(dq ,
the default, uses the absolute path of the match;
.Ql \(dqname\(dq
its last component;
.Ql { strip_prefix = \(dq/mnt/disk\(dq }
the path without that prefix; and
.Ql { literal = \(dqphotos-2019\(dq }
that fixed name.
.It Cm immutable
If true,
.Nm
assumes that the contents of the root never change.
An update indexes a group once, from its first copy that exists, and then
reuses it without reading it, until
.Fl -scan-immutable
or
.Fl -rebuild ,
or until an update finds none of its copies, which removes the group.
If the contents change anyway, transfers are still correct, but deduplicate
less until the group is indexed again.
The receiver reads blocks from any copy.
All copies of a group must have the same
.Cm exclude
patterns and
.Cm one_filesystem
setting.
The default is false.
.It Cm may_vanish
If true, the root need not exist: it gets no warning when it does not, and if a
copy of a group disappears before the update scans it, the next copy is scanned
instead.
A group whose copies are all on an unmounted disk is still removed, and indexed
again when the disk is back.
The default is false.
.It Cm one_filesystem
If true, leave out files and directories on a different mount than their
parent directory.
Unlike
.Ql rsync -x ,
this compares mounts rather than devices.
The default is false.
.El
.Pp
Sizes, in the configuration file and in options, are a number of bytes, or a
number followed by
.Ql KiB ,
.Ql MiB ,
.Ql GiB
or
.Ql TiB ,
or by
.Ql KB ,
.Ql MB ,
.Ql GB
or
.Ql TB
for powers of 1000, in any case.
In the configuration file, sizes and durations can also be integers.
.Pp
Paths, patterns and identifiers are byte strings: after TOML parsing,
.Ql \exHH
stands for a byte and
.Ql \e\e
for a backslash, and any other backslash is an error, except in patterns, where
.Ql \e* ,
.Ql \e? ,
.Ql \e[ ,
.Ql \e] ,
.Ql \e- ,
.Ql \e!
and
.Ql \e^
match those characters literally, as does a byte given as
.Ql \exHH .
Single-quoted TOML strings avoid doubling the backslashes.
In
.Cm index_path
and
.Cm tmp_data_dir ,
.Ql ~
and a leading
.Ql ~/
stand for the home directory, and other relative paths are relative to the
working directory, which on the server is the home directory.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev HOME
The home directory, under which the configuration, indexes and caches are kept
by default.
.It Ev PATH
On the server, where the remote shell finds
.Nm
and, with
.Fl s ,
where
.Nm
finds the command.
.It Ev SHELL
On the server, the shell that runs the command, or
.Pa /bin/sh
if unset.
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
Used instead of
.Pa ~/.cache .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used instead of
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
On the server, the directory of the socket that connects the two SSH sessions;
.Pa /tmp/hashsync- Ns Ar uid
if unset.
.El
.Pp
The XDG variables are only used if they hold an absolute path.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/hashsync/ Ns Ar profile Ns Pa .toml
Configuration file of a profile.
.It Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/ Ns Ar profile Ns Pa .db/
Index of a profile, with its lock file
.Pa hashsync.lock .
.It Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/account-id
Random identifier of the user's account, which the hash list caches use to tell
accounts apart.
.It Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/local-hashlists/
Cached hash lists of this host's indexes, as sent to other accounts.
.It Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/remote-hashlists/
Cached hash lists received from other accounts.
.It Pa ~/.cache/hashsync/tmp-data/
Default
.Cm tmp_data_dir .
.El
.Pp
If
.Nm
fails, it can leave temporary files whose names start with
.Ql .hashsync-
in the caches and in
.Cm tmp_data_dir ;
.Nm Cm ssh
removes them once they are older than
.Cm hashlist_max_age .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm Cm ssh
exits with the exit status of
.Ar command ,
128 plus the number of the signal that terminated it, or 127 if it could not be
started, even if the program reading its output exited early.
If
.Nm Cm ssh
itself fails, for example because SSH cannot reach the host, it exits with 1,
or with 128 plus the signal number after SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.Pp
The other commands exit with 0 on success, even if they skipped files or
directories that cannot be read, and 1 on failure.
Usage errors exit with 2.
A bug, or an error in the index database such as corruption, aborts
.Nm
with SIGABRT, which shells report as status 134.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a directory from the host
.Ql nas
over an older local copy of it, which the local index covers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hashsync ssh -n backup@nas tar -C /srv/data -cf - . |
    tar -C /srv/data -xf -
.Ed
.Pp
The same in the other direction, deduplicated against the index on
.Ql nas :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tar -C /srv/data -cf - . |
    hashsync ssh --dedup-stdin backup@nas tar -C /srv/data -xf -
.Ed
.Pp
Use
.Nm
as the remote shell of
.Xr rsync 1 ,
to pull and to push.
Deduplication is much more effective with a patched rsync
.Pq see Sx CAVEATS .
With
.Fl -delete-delay ,
rsync deletes files only after the transfer, so files that are about to be
deleted, such as the old names of renamed files, can still be used for
deduplication:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rsync -a --delete-delay -e 'hashsync ssh' nas:/srv/data/ /srv/data/
rsync -a --delete-delay -e 'hashsync ssh --dedup-stdin' \e
    /srv/data/ nas:/srv/data/
.Ed
.Pp
Run the server from a statically linked binary in the remote home directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hashsync ssh -n --hashsync-path bin/hashsync nas cat big.iso > big.iso
.Ed
.Pp
The default profile, in
.Pa ~/.config/hashsync/default.toml ,
for the data directory of the examples above:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
block_size = "16KiB"

[[roots]]
path = "/srv/data"
exclude = ["*.tmp", ".cache"]
one_filesystem = true
.Ed
.Pp
A profile for two Nix stores at separate locations, in
.Pa ~/.config/hashsync/nix.toml ,
which
.Ql --profile nix
selects:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[roots]]
path = "/nix/store/*"
immutable = true
identifier = "name"
exclude = [".links"]
may_vanish = true

[[roots]]
path = "/mnt/disk/nix/store/*"
immutable = true
identifier = "name"
exclude = [".links"]
.Ed
.Pp
A root whose name contains the byte FF, which is not valid UTF-8, and
exclusions of the literal names
.Ql literal*name
and
.Ql back\eslash :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[roots]]
path = '/srv/archive-\exFF'
immutable = true
identifier = { literal = 'archive-\exFF' }
exclude = ['*.tmp', 'literal\ex2Aname', 'back\e\eslash']
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to standard error.
Notes report progress and summaries, and
.Fl q
suppresses them; messages from the server are marked as such.
Paths and other values from outside the program are shown in the byte-string
syntax of the configuration file.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rsync 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr tar 1 ,
.Xr zstd 1 ,
.Xr ssh_config 5
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm Cm ssh
holds a lock on each index it deduplicates against until the stream has ended,
and
.Nm
commands that need exclusive access to the index wait for it.
These include
.Nm Cm update
and the index update at the start of another
.Nm Cm ssh .
While that second
.Nm Cm ssh
waits, its stream is sent without deduplication, or held back if
.Fl -wait
is given.
With
.Fl -no-update ,
several instances of
.Nm Cm ssh
can share the index.
.Pp
The lock can cause a deadlock if the
.Ar command
that
.Nm Cm ssh
runs on the server itself runs a
.Nm
command that needs exclusive access to an index, such as
.Nm Cm update .
For example,
.Ql "hashsync ssh --dedup-stdin nas 'hashsync update; tar -xf -'"
never finishes: the update waits for the lock, which is only released once
.Xr tar 1
has read all of its standard input.
Two
.Nm Cm ssh
commands in opposite directions between the same two hosts can also deadlock if
each one's
.Ar command
runs
.Nm Cm update
on its server, since each update then waits for the other
.Nm Cm ssh .
.Pp
.Xr rsync 1
sends file data in pieces of at most 32\~KiB, which makes deduplication
ineffective.
To deduplicate rsync transfers effectively, the rsync that sends the files
needs a patch that raises this limit to 8\~MiB; the patch is backwards
compatible, so the other rsync can be unpatched.
.Pp
If SSH multiplexes sessions over one connection, for example with
.Cm ControlMaster ,
.Nm Cm ssh
still works, but the sender's questions about matching blocks wait behind the
data of the streams.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The sender of a deduplicated stream learns a hash of every block in the
receiver's index, and can test whether the receiver has a block with given
contents.
In a push, the client could read the indexed files through its SSH login
anyway, but in a pull, the server learns about the client's files, so only
pull from servers that you trust.
.Fl -no-sender-cache
keeps the server from storing the client's hash lists.
